%% Async work: how a POST becomes a completed job (and what happens when
%% things fail at every point). At-least-once is the contract; idempotency
%% claims make redelivery harmless.
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as client
    participant A as api
    participant P as Postgres
    participant R as redis-queue
    participant W as worker

    C->>A: POST /v1/projects/…/reports (Idempotency-Key)
    A->>P: BEGIN
    A->>P: INSERT report_jobs (pending)
    A->>P: INSERT outbox (same tx — the job is durable when the 2xx is)
    A->>P: COMMIT
    A-->>R: best-effort BullMQ add (skip: relay catches it)
    A-->>C: 202 { jobId, statusUrl }

    Note over R,W: fast path: BullMQ delivers immediately
    R->>W: job (at-least-once)
    Note over W: claim: INSERT … ON CONFLICT (idempotency) DO NOTHING
    alt claim won
        W->>P: SET LOCAL app.tenant_id (RLS applies)
        W->>P: UPDATE report_jobs → running → completed
        W-->>R: ack
    else claim lost (already done)
        W-->>R: ack (no-op — redelivery is harmless)
    else handler threw
        W-->>R: retry with exponential backoff (3 attempts)
        alt attempts exhausted
            W->>R: add to dead-letter queue
            Note over W: operator: /dlq lists, /dlq/replay re-enqueues<br/>(payload + idempotency key untouched)
        end
    end

    Note over W: slow path: api died before the best-effort add
    W->>P: relay tick: claim batch FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
    P-->>W: committed outbox rows (lease 120s)
    W->>R: publish → settle
